dent variables (factors) on a continuous dependent variable. It is selected when the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances are met.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**:  \n1. Formulate the null hypothesis (H0: all group means are equal) and the alternative hypothesis (H1: at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculate the F-statistic using the ratio of the mean square between groups to the mean square within groups.\n3. Compare the calculated F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution table at a specified significance level (e.g., α = 0.05).\n4. If the F-statistic exceeds the critical value, reject the null hypothesis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:  \n- ANOVA can handle multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a comprehensive analysis of variance, allowing for the assessment of the impact of multiple factors and their interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:  \n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD) are required for pairwise comparisons.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**:  \n- Post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD, Bonferroni correction) for pairwise comparisons after ANOVA.\n- Non-parametric alternatives such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are violated.\n- Mixed-design ANOVA for studies involving both within-subjects and between-subjects factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**:  \nResearchers should ensure that the assumptions of ANOVA are met before applying the method. Data transformation techniques may be employed to address violations of normality or homogeneity. Additionally, sample sizes should be balanced across groups to enhance the robustness of the results.\n\n**Software Implementation**:  \nANOVA can be performed using various statistical softw